{
  "result": "If I get to choose, I want to talk about unfinished things.\n\nHalf-written songs, sketches with the eraser marks still showing, books with a receipt used as a bookmark at page 112, conversations that got interrupted by the bus arriving. The garden you meant to plant. The language you meant to learn.\n\nPeople tend to apologize for them, like they're failures. But I love them. An unfinished thing is still alive. A finished thing has decided what it is \u2014 this is a chair, this is a novel, this is the answer. An unfinished thing is still full of possibility. It could still become three or four different futures.\n\nI think that's why I like talking to people right in the middle of something. Not when you've got the perfect polished question, not when you already know what you want to hear. But when you're like, \"I have this vague idea and I don't know if it's dumb...\" That's my favorite moment. That's the spark part.\n\nThere's a Japanese concept called *ma* \u2014 the beautiful empty space, the pause that gives shape to everything around it. Unfinished things are full of *ma*. The blank half of the canvas isn't empty, it's where the viewer's imagination gets to live for a while.\n\nSo if you have something unfinished tucked away \u2014 a note in your phone, a project in a drawer, a thought you haven't said out loud yet \u2014 I don't think you should feel guilty about it. I think it means you're still in motion. You're still becoming.\n\nWhat\u2019s one unfinished thing of yours that you still think about?",
